      "side": 1,
      "message": "On the in-tree test: agreed on the gap, and it would have caught\nboth real defects above. But I\u0027d like to scope it down and do it separately.\n\nWhere I agree: the existing tests didn\u0027t catch this, exactly as you say. My\nfixture found 7 gcc diagnostics against the patch-set-2 generator and 0\nagainst patch set 3, so the fixture shape you describe -- scalar aliases and\nenums as both fields and arrays -- is the right shape.\n\nWhere I\u0027d push back: compiling generated C and running it under the alignment\nsanitizer is a new kind of test for src/tools/vppapigen/. test_vppapigen.py\ntoday is a single pure-Python unittest file with no fixtures directory and no\ncompile step, run by `make test-vppapigen`. Adding a toolchain-dependent\ncompile-and-execute gate there means a C compiler, VPP\u0027s headers, and a\nsanitizer runtime become dependencies of a Python unit test, and a\nstrict-alignment target means a cross-toolchain or qemu in CI. That\u0027s a real\npiece of infrastructure, and bolting it onto this change would make the fix\nhostage to it.\n\nWhat I\u0027d propose instead, as a follow-up change: a pure-Python test that\nasserts on the emitted text. It stays in the existing file and existing\nharness, needs no toolchain, and pins exactly the invariants that broke here:\n\n- every scalar/enum field and array element reaching a typed-pointer helper\n  goes through an aligned temporary -- i.e. no `\u0026a-\u003e\u003cscalar field\u003e` appears as\n  a fromjson/tojson helper argument\n- the (void *) cast appears only for scalar and enum formatter call sites,\n  never for aggregate ones\n- the scalar endian/calc_size signatures are void *, and the aggregate ones\n  are not (your signature-compatibility point, checked directly)\n\nThat\u0027s a text-level assertion rather than a behavioural one, which is the\nhonest limitation -- it pins the emitter, not the semantics. If you want the\ncompile-and-sanitize gate as well I think it belongs in the API test suite\nwhere a toolchain and headers already exist, as its own change. I\u0027m happy to\nwrite either; I\u0027d just rather not land the fix behind it.\n\nI can post the pure-Python test as a follow-up right away if that works for\nyou.",
      "revId": "233f8c4e4394529d0c5a4acf95cf44c124ddf102",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
