      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch, fixed -- and it was worse than one path.\n\nThe alias fromjson does write through a typed pointer, so\npipe_create_reply.pipe_sw_if_index[2] at offset 14 (2 mod 4) was exactly the\nbug this change was supposed to remove. Now covered for both the\nfixed-length branch and the VLA branch, via the same aligned temporary the\nscalar field path uses.\n\nChasing it with your fixture shape turned up a third site you didn\u0027t\nmention: ToJSON.print_array. get_json_array_func() returns \"\u0026\" for a scalar\nalias, while get_json_func() resolves the alias to its base type and passes\nby value -- so the array variant took the address and the field variant\ndidn\u0027t. gcc flags it; that\u0027s fixed the same way (line 253).\n\nFor the record, the two array paths that were already fine: endianfun_array\nroutes through the void * helper, and Printfun\u0027s loop was already casting.\n\nOne thing I deliberately did NOT change while in there. For a scalar alias,\ntojson emits a JSON *string* (the alias _tojson calls format_c_string) while\nthe field path emits a *number*, and the alias _fromjson reads a number via\nvl_api_u32_fromjson. So scalar-alias arrays don\u0027t round-trip through JSON\ntoday, independently of alignment. Making the array path consistent with the\nfield path would fix it but changes the JSON representation, so it wants its\nown change and its own discussion. I kept the aligned-temporary form that\npreserves current output byte-for-byte.",
