      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d like to push back on this one, with numbers.\n\nThe signature change is real, but I think the exposure is narrower than it\nlooks:\n\n- Registration can\u0027t break. vl_msg_api_msg_config_t types every handler slot\n  as plain void * (api_common.h:107-118), and the canonical types in\n  api_main_t are already void (*endian_handler) (void *, bool) and\n  uword (*calc_size_func) (void *) (api_common.h:228,231). This change moves\n  scalar helpers toward that declared contract, not away from it.\n- Only message helpers are ever registered. Scalar alias and enum helpers are\n  called directly from generated code. Taking a typed function pointer to\n  vl_api_interface_index_t_endian is expressible, but grep finds zero\n  instances in tree, and every .endian \u003d ... site I can find is a message.\n- The formatters need a caveat, and here your concern has more force than the\n  rest of it. There are no hand-written *callers* of a scalar or enum\n  formatter in tree. But there are hand-written *implementations* that\n  override generated ones for scalar aliases, and one class of them is\n  already out of sync with the generated call sites, independently of this\n  change: format_vl_api_timestamp_t (jsonformat.c:436) reads\n  va_arg (*args, f64) -- a value -- while the generated call site has always\n  passed the field\u0027s address. vpe.api\u0027s vpe_system_time, start_timestamp and\n  timestamp all go through it, so that pretty-printer has been reinterpreting\n  a pointer as an f64 for a long time; vpe_types.api_tojson.h does the same\n  through format_c_string. timedelta forwards to it and has the same problem.\n\n  My change rewrites that call site from `\u0026a-\u003ex` to `(void *) \u0026a-\u003ex`, so it\n  neither fixes nor worsens it -- both spellings mismatch va_arg(f64). I\u0027ve\n  left it alone deliberately: it\u0027s a different bug in a hand-written file,\n  and the fix is to give the formatter a pointer parameter like every other\n  one, which wants its own change. But it is a concrete instance of exactly\n  the hazard you\u0027re pointing at, so I don\u0027t want to wave the formatter\n  question away. Out-of-tree I can\u0027t prove a negative either, which is why\n  the caveat stays in the commit message.\n- The full debug tree builds warning-free and error-free on patch set 3\n  (1695 targets), which covers every consumer of these helpers in tree.\n\nOn the u32u alternative: it does exist (vppinfra/types.h:162) and it would\npreserve the signatures, but I think it\u0027s the riskier of the two. You flagged\nthe reason yourself -- it changes _Alignof(vl_api_\u003ctype\u003e_t), which changes\nlayout in any consumer-defined struct embedding one of these types. That\u0027s a\nsilent ABI change in third-party code, which seems strictly worse than a\nsignature change on helpers that nothing outside generated code appears to\ntake pointers to. It also doesn\u0027t extend cleanly to enums.\n\nSo my preference is to keep void *. If you\u0027re still uncomfortable, the middle\nground I\u0027d suggest is keeping the public typed helpers as thin inline wrappers\nand having generated call sites use internal unaligned accessors -- happy to\ndo that instead, it\u0027s just more generated code for a case I can\u0027t find a\ncaller for. Your call.",
